{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789339489,"nonce":"eCkU89b9rcFpu6auxULcGidsYhI=","title":"Python: an override returning another type than the base method miscompiles the dispatcher","message":"class A:\n        def f(self): return 1\n    class B(A):\n        def f(self): raise ValueError(\"b\")\n    def via_base(x: A): return x.f()\n    try: print(via_base(B()))\n    except ValueError as e: print(\"caught\", e)\n\nprints \"TypeError: 'NoneType' object cannot be interpreted as an integer\"\n(CPython: \"caught b\"). Through a dynamic receiver the ValueError is lost\nand None is printed.\n\n`A$f$dispatch` is declared with `A.f`'s signature (ret Int) and coerces\neach override's result to it. `B.f` returns None (it only raises), so\nthe dispatcher reads the placeholder back as an int, and that checked\nread raises a TypeError over the pending ValueError. The same happens\nwith any override whose return type differs from the base's: a str\nreturned where the base returns int is read as an int.\n\nFix: the signature a dispatched method is called through is the join of\nevery implementation's return type in the hierarchy (Object where they\ndiffer), so the dispatcher boxes rather than converts; and a dispatcher\nmust not coerce the result of a call that left an exception pending.\n\nPinned by conformance/exceptions/override_return_types.py in\nKNOWN_FAILURES.","files":null}]}